--TEST--
The prefix and the captures are one member of `Core\Request` and it always answers, where the route the same request carries may be `null` -- `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler` and `rule:routing/a-request-reads-its-mount`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 7 replaced `mountPrefix`, which stated half of the fact. Two members for
// one answer is what this repository's one-home rule forbids, and nothing was
// ever built on the old spelling, so nothing migrated -- the refusal at the
// bottom is what pins that there is no second way to ask.
//
// Beside it, the boundary the sibling reader draws differently. The binding
// below names no `?`, so a `mount()` that answered `null` the way `route()`
// does would be a second error here: nothing matching the route table is an
// ordinary served request, but every request that reached this program reached
// it through some mount, and a door that strips nothing carries `""` and no
// captures -- an answer, not an absence.
Core\Request\Mount $mount = Core\Request::mount();
tainted string $tenant = $mount->captures()[0];
echo $mount->prefix(), " ", $tenant, "\n";
var $half = Core\Request::mountPrefix();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Request` has no member named `mountPrefix`
   --> case.nvs:16:13
